## 3. API Design

### 3.1 Function Family and Naming Conventions

The API follows Seurat's established `Find*` family pattern:

| Seurat Pattern | Our Function | Comparison Type | Analogy |
|---|---|---|---|
| `FindMarkers` | `FindSimilarity` | One cluster vs. one/rest | Targeted comparison |
| `FindAllMarkers` | `FindAllSimilarity` | All pairwise clusters | Exhaustive pairwise |
| `FindConservedMarkers` | `FindConservedSimilarity` | Between clusters, per condition | Cross-condition stability |
| *(no analogue)* | `FindDivergence` | Within cluster, across conditions | Condition shift |

### 3.2 FindSimilarity — Core Function

This is the fundamental building block. All other functions in the family call FindSimilarity internally or share its core computation engine.

**Signature:**

```r
FindSimilarity(
  object,                # Seurat object
  ident.1 = NULL,        # Reference cluster identity
  ident.2 = NULL,        # Comparison cluster (NULL = all others)
  reduction = "pca",     # Embedding to use
  dims = NULL,           # Dimensions (NULL = auto-detect)
  method = "euclidean",  # Distance metric
  group.by = NULL,       # Override active idents
  features = NULL,       # Gene-space mode (alternative to reduction)
  assay = NULL,          # Assay for gene-space mode
  slot = "data",         # Slot for gene-space mode
  weight.by.var = FALSE, # Weight dims by variance explained
  n.perm = 0,            # Permutation tests (0 = off)
  min.cells = 10,        # Minimum cells per group
  seed = 42,             # Random seed for reproducibility
  verbose = TRUE,        # Progress messages
  ...                    # Method-specific arguments
)
```

**Return:** A data frame with columns: `cluster`, `distance`, `n.cells.1`, `n.cells.2`, `rank`, and optionally `p.value`, `p.value.adj.bonf`, `p.value.adj.bh`. Sorted by distance ascending (most similar first).

### 3.3 FindAllSimilarity

Iterates FindSimilarity over all clusters, producing a full pairwise distance matrix. Analogous to `FindAllMarkers`.

**Signature:**

```r
FindAllSimilarity(
  object,
  reduction = "pca",
  dims = NULL,
  method = "euclidean",
  group.by = NULL,
  return.matrix = TRUE,  # TRUE = dist matrix, FALSE = long table
  ...                    # Passed to FindSimilarity
)
```

**Return:** When `return.matrix = TRUE`, a symmetric distance matrix (class `dist` or `matrix`). When `FALSE`, a long-format data frame with columns: `cluster.1`, `cluster.2`, `distance`, `rank`.

### 3.4 FindConservedSimilarity

For a given pair of clusters, computes their distance within each level of a grouping variable, then tests whether the distance is conserved across conditions. Analogous to `FindConservedMarkers`.

**Signature:**

```r
FindConservedSimilarity(
  object,
  ident.1,
  ident.2 = NULL,
  grouping.var,          # Metadata column to stratify by
  reduction = "pca",
  method = "euclidean",
  meta.method = "fisher",# How to combine per-condition results
  ...
)
```

**Return:** Data frame with per-condition distances, a combined/meta-analytic p-value, and a measure of cross-condition variance in distance.

### 3.5 FindDivergence

Measures within-cluster shifts across conditions. For each cluster, splits cells by a metadata variable and computes the distance between condition sub-populations. Answers: "How much does this cluster change between conditions?"

**Signature:**

```r
FindDivergence(
  object,
  group.by,              # Metadata column (e.g., "condition")
  ident.1 = NULL,        # Condition A (NULL = all pairwise)
  ident.2 = NULL,        # Condition B
  split.by = NULL,       # Cluster variable (default: active idents)
  reduction = "pca",
  method = "wasserstein",# Default to distributional for this use case
  n.perm = 0,
  ...
)
```

**Return:** Data frame with columns: `cluster`, `condition.1`, `condition.2`, `divergence`, `n.cells.1`, `n.cells.2`, `p.value` (if `n.perm > 0`). Sorted by divergence descending (most shifted clusters first).

