## 4. Internal Architecture

### 4.1 Execution Pipeline

Every function in the FindSimilarity family follows a common internal pipeline with six stages:

**Stage 1 — Identity Resolution.** Resolve active identities, optionally overridden by `group.by`. Validate that `ident.1` and `ident.2` exist. Retrieve the mapping from cell barcodes to cluster labels. This follows the same pattern as FindMarkers' internal `.prep_identities` step.

**Stage 2 — Embedding Extraction.** Extract the embedding matrix. In reduction mode (default), pull from the specified reduction slot and subset to the requested dims. In gene-space mode (when `features` is provided), extract from the assay data slot and transpose to cells-by-features. If `dims` is NULL, auto-detect: first check if the object stores `nn.dims` from a prior `FindNeighbors` call; if not, use all available dimensions in the reduction.

**Stage 3 — Cell Partitioning.** Split the embedding matrix into per-group subsets based on the resolved identities. Apply `min.cells` filtering: any group with fewer cells than the threshold is flagged with a warning and excluded from comparisons.

**Stage 4 — Representation Computation.** Depending on the distance method type (centroid-based, distribution-based, or graph-based), compute the required per-group representation. For centroid methods: column means of the embedding submatrix. For distribution methods: retain the full cell-level embedding. For graph-based methods: extract relevant edges from the kNN graph stored in the Seurat object.

**Stage 5 — Distance Computation.** Dispatch to the appropriate distance function via the method registry (see Section 5). Iterate over all requested comparisons. Optionally apply variance weighting to dimensions before computation.

**Stage 6 — Statistical Testing (optional).** If `n.perm > 0`, run permutation testing by shuffling cluster labels within the pooled cell set, recomputing the distance under the null, and deriving empirical p-values. Apply multiple testing correction (Bonferroni and BH) when multiple comparisons are made.

### 4.2 Method Registry Pattern

Distance methods are organised in an internal registry that maps method names to their computation type (centroid, distribution, or graph) and their implementing function. This makes the framework extensible: adding a new distance metric requires only registering it in the list without modifying the core pipeline.

Each registered method also declares its additional requirements (e.g., Mahalanobis needs the raw data for covariance estimation even though it is centroid-based; graph-based methods need a pre-computed nearest-neighbour graph), which the pipeline checks before dispatching.

```r
# Conceptual structure of the method registry
similarity.methods <- list(
  euclidean   = list(type = "centroid",      fn = EuclideanDistance),
  cosine      = list(type = "centroid",      fn = CosineDistance),
  correlation = list(type = "centroid",      fn = CorrelationDistance),
  mahalanobis = list(type = "centroid",      fn = MahalanobisDistance,  needs.data = TRUE),
  wasserstein = list(type = "distribution",  fn = SlicedWassersteinDistance),
  energy      = list(type = "distribution",  fn = EnergyDistance),
  mmd         = list(type = "distribution",  fn = MMDDistance),
  knn_overlap = list(type = "graph",         fn = KNNOverlapDistance,   needs.graph = TRUE)
)
```

### 4.3 Variance Weighting

When `weight.by.var = TRUE` and the reduction is PCA (or any reduction with stored standard deviations / variance explained), each dimension of the embedding is scaled by the square root of its variance explained before distance computation. This gives PCs with higher eigenvalues more influence on the distance, reflecting the biological signal structure. This is an empirical design decision that must be validated (see Section 7, Experiment 1).

### 4.4 Permutation Testing

The null hypothesis is that cluster labels are exchangeable. For a comparison between cluster A (nA cells) and cluster B (nB cells), the permutation procedure pools all nA + nB cells, randomly reassigns nA of them to the "pseudo-A" group and nB to "pseudo-B", then recomputes the distance. Repeating this `n.perm` times yields a null distribution. The empirical p-value is `(number of null distances >= observed distance + 1) / (n.perm + 1)`.

For `FindAllSimilarity` (many pairwise comparisons) and `FindDivergence` (many clusters), p-values are adjusted using both Bonferroni and Benjamini-Hochberg, reported as separate columns.

